BusyBox-rs

Please see the LICENSE file for details on copying and usage.
Please refer to the INSTALL file for instructions on how to build.

What is BusyBox-rs:

  BusyBox-rs is a modular reimplementation of BusyBox in Rust, combining
  compact versions of common UNIX utilities into a single executable.
  It provides replacements for coreutils, fileutils, findutils, editors,
  and networking tools while aiming for behavior parity with upstream BusyBox.

  Written with size-optimization and minimal dependencies in mind (only libc
  at the low level), BusyBox-rs allows individual applets to be enabled or
  disabled at compile time using Cargo features.

----------------

Using BusyBox-rs:

  The behavior of BusyBox-rs is determined by the name it is called under:
  as "cp" it behaves like cp, as "sed" it behaves like sed, and so on.
  Called directly as "busybox" it takes the applet name as the first argument:

    busybox ls -la /proc

  You can also create symlinks to the binary for each applet:

    ln -s busybox ls
    ./ls -la /proc

----------------

Building BusyBox-rs with Rust & musl-libc:

  Requirements:
    - Rust toolchain (Rust Edition 2024 supported)
    - cargo
    - target x86_64-unknown-linux-musl (for static standalone deployment)

  Standard release build:
    make

  Static musl build (recommended for embedded deployment):
    rustup target add x86_64-unknown-linux-musl
    cargo build --release --target x86_64-unknown-linux-musl

  Configuration targets:
    make defconfig      # Standard profile with all 410 supported applets
    make tinyconfig     # Minimal embedded profile (echo, cat, ls, ash)
    make allnoconfig    # Bare configuration

  Installation:
    make install DESTDIR=/target/path CONFIG_PREFIX=/usr

  Testing:
    make check          # Upstream regression testsuite execution
    make bloatcheck     # Binary size and section inspection
